title: Counting to seven
mode: draw
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
parts: dff and not chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
Three flip-flops count from 0 to 7. The pattern goes on from the two-bit counter: a bit flips when **all** the bits to its right are 1.

- `q0` flips at every tick
- `q1` flips when `q0` is 1
- `q2` flips when `q1` **and** `q0` are 1

Look at 3 → 4: 011 becomes 100. All three bits flip, because the two on the right were 1.
---- task
Make `q2 q1 q0` count 0, 1, 2 ... 7 and round to 0.
---- start
clock clk 0 380
led q0 600 40
led q1 600 180
led q2 600 320
---- reference
clock clk
led q0, q1, q2
wire a[1], b[1], c[1]
a = dff(~a, clk)
b = dff(b ^ a, clk)
c = dff(c ^ (b & a), clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
q2 = c
---- answer
clock clk 0 380
not n0 160 0
dff f0 160 60
chip x1 my_xor 300 170
dff f1 440 180
and g2 280 300
chip x2 my_xor 380 310
dff f2 480 320
led q0 600 40
led q1 600 180
led q2 600 320
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> n0.in
n0 -> f0.d
f0.q -> q0
f1.q -> x1.a
f0.q -> x1.b
x1.y -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
f1.q -> g2.0
f0.q -> g2.1
f2.q -> x2.a
g2 -> x2.b
x2.y -> f2.d
clk -> f2.clk
f2.q -> q2
---- hint1
Build the two-bit counter first. The third bit is a flip-flop that takes `q2` XOR something.
---- hint2
That something is `q1` AND `q0`: an AND gate into the second my_xor, with the third flip-flop's own `q` as its other input.
---- solution
Bits 0 and 1 are your counter. Bit 2 takes `q2` XOR (`q1` AND `q0`), so it flips only on the way from 3 to 4 and from 7 to 0.
---- done
Each new bit needs one more AND and one more XOR. A 32-bit counter is this pattern 32 times, and it counts to over four billion.
